## 📖 What I Learned

### ☁️ 1. Cloud Computing & AWS
Cloud Computing means using computing resources (servers, storage, databases) 
over the internet instead of owning physical hardware. AWS (Amazon Web Services) 
is the largest cloud provider offering these resources on a pay-as-you-go basis.

**Service Models:**

| Model | Full Form | AWS Manages | You Manage | Example |
|-------|-----------|-------------|------------|---------|
| IaaS | Infrastructure as a Service | Hardware | OS & Apps | EC2 |
| PaaS | Platform as a Service | Infra + Runtime | Just Code | Elastic Beanstalk |
| SaaS | Software as a Service | Everything | Nothing | Gmail |

### 🔐 2. AWS Account Setup
Created a Free Tier AWS account. Free Tier gives limited free usage for 
**12 months** on services like EC2, S3, and Lambda — but usage must be 
monitored to avoid unexpected billing.

### 👤 3. IAM (Identity and Access Management)
IAM controls **who** can access **what** in your AWS account.

| Term | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| **Root User** | Created during sign-up, full access — use only for account-level tasks |
| **IAM User** | Individual identity for daily use, with limited permissions |
| **IAM Role** | Temporary set of permissions, not tied to one person |
| **IAM Policy** | JSON document defining exact allowed/denied actions |

> **Flow:** `User/Role` → attached `Policy` → defines access → to AWS `Resource`

